[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router0
diagnosis_text:
The broken component is dynamic routing (RIP) between router1 and router0, not router1’s WAN link. From the Office/Principle side, router1 lacks routes to the CS and IT subnets, so it immediately rejects that traffic. Evidence: traceroute from office_pc to 192.168.1.2 returns “192.168.3.1 … !N” at the first hop, meaning router1 has no route to that network. On router1, the kernel FIB lacks 192.168.1.0/24 and 192.168.2.0/24 (ip route show), confirming why router1 emits ICMP net-unreachable.

The uplink itself is healthy. router1’s eth2 (10.10.0.2/8) to router0 (10.10.0.1/8) is UP and passes traffic: ping from router1 to 10.10.0.1 succeeds with low RTT. Traffic to other remote networks via router2 still works (e.g., 128.168.0.0/16), showing router1’s routing and forwarding are otherwise fine.

Localization and root cause: router0 is not advertising its connected CS/IT networks. Its FRR running config contains no RIP stanza at all, so router1 never learns 192.168.1.0/24 and 192.168.2.0/24 via eth2. Thus, the user-visible symptom is caused by missing routes on router1 due to router0’s absent RIP, not a failing router1 uplink.

Corrective fix: restore/enable RIP on router0 and advertise 192.168.1.0/24, 192.168.2.0/24, and 10.0.0.0/8 (or add equivalent static routes on router1 pointing to 10.10.0.1). Verify router1’s routing table then includes those prefixes and that office/principle traceroutes no longer show !N at 192.168.3.1. The affected interface (suspected uplink) is router1 eth2, but it is operating normally. 
=== END ===